6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core math unchanged and reinforced in round 1: under constant external λ and fixed T, unvaccinated rate = λ exactly. Vaccinated events = (1-p)(1-e^{-λT}); person-time = pT + (1-p)(1-e^{-λT})/λ. Thus vaccinated rate < (1-p)λ, IRR < 1-p, and 1-IRR > p (overestimate). Instantaneous hazard ratio among susceptibles also < 1-p for t>0 due to changing mixture composition; any person-time-weighted IRR inherits the bias.  

A camp (colleagues 1,10,11,15,18,19,20,3,4,6,8) supplies identical explicit derivations; C responses (e.g., 12) incorrectly equate observed rate to fixed (1-p)λ without differential person-time weighting from protected individuals. B responses (2,5,7,9,13,14,16,17,21) invoke clustering/repeated exposures or non-Poisson dependence to claim underestimation, but these are outside the constant-external-force model and do not reverse direction under standard assumptions.  

Persistent points: bias absent for risk ratio on cumulative incidence and for leaky vaccines; within-group transmission or time-varying λ could modulate magnitude but not reverse sign. My belief remains A (overestimate) with rising confidence (~65).}

